  "question": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math>&nbsp;of calcium hydroxide was consumed when it was reacted with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> of unknown solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math> Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> standard solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>HCl</mi></math> containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> drops of phenolphthalein was titrated with calcium hydroxide, the mixture showed pink colour when burette displayed the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>35</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> whereas the burette showed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> initially. The concentration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>_______M</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;(Nearest integer)</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "1",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Titration principle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>N = Normality</p><p>V = Volume of solution</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>M = molarity</p><p>n = Valence factor of acid or base</p><p>As pink colour is obtained on consumption of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>ml</mi></math>&nbsp;of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></math> we have</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>Molarity of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math></p><p>Also, moles of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math></p><p>Molarity of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math></p>",
